#
# WHY A SECOND SAMPLE AT ALL. article-topic.jsonl's real rows are the PER-SYMBOL
# feed, where a story about Costco says "Costco" rather than "retailers".
# /headlines serves the general feed, where the subject is usually named. The
# two populations score very differently and neither one is wrong; what is wrong
# is quoting one number without its sample.
#
#   58%  general feed, 50 headlines, FITTED -- the table was written on them
#   33%  general feed, 42 headlines, held out  <- this file
#   6.8% per-symbol feed, 192 headlines, held out
#
# `description` is the card excerpt AS RENDERED AND TRUNCATED AT ~100 CHARACTERS
# by the capture. Rule 3 wants two occurrences in the description, so that leg
# fires less here than it will in production. 33% is a floor on that account.
#
# `imprecise` marks a row whose output is defensible but not good; not asserted,
# same convention as article-topic.jsonl. `note` marks a recall miss, kept as a
# negative with the phrase that would have reached it, so a later widening has
# to argue with a recorded case rather than a hunch.
